        "note_tweet": {
          "text": "Three years ago, I was confident that we were on the verge of mass adoption of consumer crypto, so I dove into the work I’m doing now.\n\nBut recently, I realized that it’s not just consumer crypto we’re pushing the limits for. The somewhat cliched notion of “mass adoption” that we’ve been referring to over the past five years is, in essence, a completely new lifestyle.\n\nIt’s a lifestyle for sovereign individuals, made possible by infrastructure like digital payment rails that allow you to live well in the real world and digital identities that enable you to travel freely with minimal barriers.\n\nIt’s a lifestyle where one’s expression and creations can be curated by others, but ultimately are controlled by oneself.\n\nIt’s a lifestyle where only the “current” and “now” matter, because the influence of anyone or anything is reflected almost in real-time in what we’re experiencing. The concept of an end result is nearly obsolete, as the world has truly become an infinite game.\n\nSo, everything each of us is building—the IDs, the payment systems, the decentralized social rails, the smart-contract-backed gamified mechanisms—are not the end goals. They are means to achieving a life for sovereign individuals.\n\nEntering the era that “High Tech, Low Life” cyberpunk literature once described, I am convinced that we can afford both High Tech and High Life."
        },
